WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.778
Safe query construction is the first line
of defense,

2
00:00:02.858 --> 00:00:04.832
but it should be paired with damage
reduction.

3
00:00:04.912 --> 00:00:06.785
Software changes, configuration changes,

4
00:00:06.865 --> 00:00:09.295
and unusual feature paths can introduce
mistakes later.

5
00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:12.919
Least privilege makes those mistakes less
costly by limiting what the application

6
00:00:12.999 --> 00:00:14.518
account can do inside the database.

7
00:00:14.598 --> 00:00:18.192
A runtime service account should have only
the permissions needed for the service's

8
00:00:18.272 --> 00:00:18.829
normal work.

9
00:00:18.909 --> 00:00:22.554
A read-only reporting endpoint should not
use credentials that can modify unrelated

10
00:00:22.634 --> 00:00:22.988
tables.

11
00:00:23.068 --> 00:00:26.308
A background worker that updates a small
set of records should not need broad

12
00:00:26.388 --> 00:00:27.653
administrative privileges.

13
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:29.303
When roles match responsibilities,

14
00:00:29.383 --> 00:00:32.926
an unsafe query path has fewer
opportunities to affect data outside its legitimate

15
00:00:33.006 --> 00:00:33.310
scope.

16
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:36.427
Migration roles, maintenance roles,
support roles, analytics roles,

17
00:00:36.507 --> 00:00:39.443
and runtime application roles should be
separated where practical.

18
00:00:39.523 --> 00:00:42.915
The account that creates tables or changes
schema should not be the same account

19
00:00:42.995 --> 00:00:44.413
used by a public request handler.

20
00:00:44.493 --> 00:00:47.682
Production roles should also be distinct
from development and test roles,

21
00:00:47.762 --> 00:00:50.496
because convenience permissions often grow
during local work.

22
00:00:50.576 --> 00:00:53.866
Permissions are not a replacement for
parameterized queries or allowlisted

23
00:00:53.946 --> 00:00:54.452
structure.

24
00:00:54.532 --> 00:00:55.798
They are a containment layer.

25
00:00:55.878 --> 00:00:57.194
They also improve operations:

26
00:00:57.274 --> 00:01:00.615
unusual permission errors can reveal that
a code path is trying to do something

27
00:01:00.695 --> 00:01:02.366
outside its expected responsibility.

28
00:01:02.446 --> 00:01:04.876
When teams design database permissions
intentionally,

29
00:01:04.956 --> 00:01:07.790
they create another reviewable boundary
around the application.

30
00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:10.199
Damage reduction is most useful when it is
documented.

31
00:01:10.279 --> 00:01:12.405
Developers should know which role a
service uses,

32
00:01:12.485 --> 00:01:13.903
which tables it can read or write,

33
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.311
and which operational tasks require
elevated access.

34
00:01:16.391 --> 00:01:19.733
Clear ownership keeps least privilege from
becoming a mystery that slows down

35
00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:20.420
maintenance.
